# HW-021b - why the OBVIOUS fix for the leak is WRONG. 2026-07-12, COM35.
#
# This is the patch I submitted upstream (zephyr#113311) and then had to withdraw.
# It simply returns the in-flight block in *_stop_transfer(). On hardware it CRASHES
# within two cycles:
#
#   FATAL EXCEPTION: EXCCAUSE 28 (load prohibited)
#   PC   k_mem_slab_alloc      (kernel/mem_slab.c:245)
#   from i2s_esp32_rx_callback (drivers/i2s/i2s_esp32.c)
#   VADDR 0x220022   <- 0x0022, 0x0022: two int16 audio samples, not a pointer
#
# mem_slab.c:245 is `slab->free_list = *(char **)(slab->free_list);`. The free list's
# next pointer has been overwritten with captured audio.
#
# THE CANARY MEASURES IT EXACTLY. Stamp all eight free RX blocks with 0x5A, hand them
# back, wait 50 ms, take them again: EXACTLY ONE comes back with a stray write in it,
# every cycle. (The first version of the canary said all eight, which was MY BUG:
# k_mem_slab_free() stores the free-list next pointer in the first word of the block
# itself, so checking from offset 0 counts the slab doing its job as damage.)
#
# So the DMA is still writing into memory the driver has already returned to the slab.
# On the SOC_GDMA_SUPPORTED path, *_stop_transfer() calls dma_stop() and NOTHING else --
# it never stops the I2S unit feeding the DMA, unlike the non-GDMA branch three lines
# below it. THE LEAK HAS BEEN MASKING THAT: the block was never given back, so the
# stray writes landed in memory nobody would look at again.
